  "question": "The input has three sections. [CARD] is the requirement card a human holds: what the human wants and the answers the human has settled on. [SCREENS] is what the finished app declares: its name and description, and each screen with its key, label and the sizes it is drawn at. [SCREEN_SOURCE] is the source code of the app's screens (the front-end files under src/screens, possibly cut off). Decide whether the screen design fits the app's purpose for this person. Look at what the person would actually see and do on the screens, not at how the code is written. For a calendar, a screen that fits lets the person see their schedule laid out by date (for example a month, week or day view, or a day-by-day agenda with dates) and work from it, and carries what the card says the person needs (adding, viewing and removing, reminders, and any model or behaviour the card settles, such as recurring definitions drawn virtually or an occurrence turned into a stored item when it gets an action). A screen that is only a generic list or table of records with an add form and delete buttons, with no date or calendar layout, does not fit. Verdicts: fits = the screens give the person a purpose-fitting view and the main things on the card can be done from them; generic = the screens are a generic list or form that would look the same for any kind of app, or a main thing on the card cannot be done from the screens; cannot_tell = the input does not let you decide (for example no screens or no source). The quote must be the part of the input that decided your verdict (for fits, the declared screen or the source line that shows the date or calendar layout; for generic, the screen or source line that shows the plain list or the missing layout).",
  "verdicts": [
    "fits",
    "generic",
    "cannot_tell"
  ]
}

{
  "name": "app-design",
  "pass": [
    "fits"
  ],
  "question": "The input has three sections. [CARD] is the requirement card a human holds: what the human wants and the answers the human has settled on. [JOURNEYS] and [SCREENS] are those two sections of DESIGN.md, the design the App Engineer wrote before building the app; either may say it is missing. Judge the design as written, not code. First find the main screen: the screen the app opens on (if the design does not say, the first screen it describes). Decide whether it is the screen that well-known apps of the same kind open on. For a calendar that is a grid of dates, such as a month or week view laid out by date; a list, a table, an agenda or a list grouped by day or week is not the main screen of a calendar, even when it shows dates. For other kinds of app, a list or table of records is the main screen only when well-known apps of that kind open on such a list. Then decide whether each thing the person wants on the card (for example adding, viewing and removing, and reminders) has a journey done on the main screen or on a screen reached from it. Something the card leaves to the agent's recommendation does not need to appear. Verdicts: fits = the main screen is the one well-known apps of this kind open on and every wanted thing on the card has a journey from it; wrong_main_screen = the main screen is not the one well-known apps of this kind open on; task_missing = the main screen fits but a wanted thing on the card has no journey or no place on the screens; cannot_tell = the input does not let you decide (for example both sections are missing). The quote must be the part of the input that decided your verdict (for fits and wrong_main_screen, the words that name the main screen and its layout; for task_missing, the card's words for the missing task).",
  "verdicts": [
    "fits",
    "wrong_main_screen",
    "task_missing",
    "cannot_tell"
  ]
}
